{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 0, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman from the Crusader states born in 1140 and died in 1187. He was the count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death. His parents were Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. In 1153 Raymond III married Constance of Antioch with whom he had one daughter Melisende.\n\n### Early life and reign\n\nRaymond III was born in 1140. Upon the death of his father in 1152, he became the count of Tripoli at the age of 12. During his reign, he allied with the Byzantine Empire against Nur ad-Din, the Zengid emir of Aleppo and Mosul. In 1158, Raymond III joined the unsuccessful Second Crusade led by Emperor Manuel I Komnenos against Nur ad-Din.\n\n### *Highlighted section part 1: Conflict with the Kingdom of Jerusalem*\n\nIn 1153, Raymond III married Constance, daughter of Prince Bohemond II of Antioch and widow of Prince Raymond of Antioch. This marriage led to conflict with the Kingdom of Jerusalem, as King Baldwin III of Jerusalem claimed the right to approve the marriage of the heiress to the Principality of Antioch. The conflict was resolved in 1156 when Raymond III renounced his claim to Antioch.\n\n### *Highlighted section part 2: Battle of Harim*\n\nIn 1164, Raymond III joined forces with Bohemond III of Antioch and the Kingdom of Jerusalem to confront Nur ad-Din. The resulting Battle of Harim was a disaster for the Crusaders, with Raymond III being captured and imprisoned by Nur ad-Din. He was released in 1173 after paying a large ransom.\n\n### *Highlighted section part 3: Third Crusade*\n\nRaymond III played a role in the Third Crusade, which was launched in response to the capture of Jerusalem by Saladin in 1187. He died during the siege of Acre in 1187. His death marked the end of the counts of Tripoli from the House of Poitiers-Toulouse, as his only child, Melisende, had died without issue in 1161.\n\nOverall, Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a significant figure in the history of the Crusader states. His reign was marked by conflict with the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Byzantine Empire, as well as his capture and imprisonment by Nur ad-Din. He played a role in the Third Crusade and his death marked the end of the counts of Tripoli from the House of Poitiers-Toulouse.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0003083468764089048}, {"token": "III", "logprob": -0.00011312322021694854}, {"token": "Count", "logprob": -0.277972012758255}, {"token": "of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "Tri", "logprob": -1.4781842764932662e-05}, {"token": "pol", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "i", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "was", "logprob": -0.10666465759277344}, {"token": "a", "logprob": -1.5310908555984497}, {"token": "noble", "logprob": -0.10586104542016983}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.004170174244791269}, {"token": "from", "logprob": -1.1461677551269531}, {"token": "the", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "Cr", "logprob": -1.012233018875122}, {"token": "us", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": "ader", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "states", "logprob": -0.0387820266187191}, {"token": "born", "logprob": -2.246150016784668}, {"token": "in", "logprob": -0.012547007761895657}, {"token": "", "logprob": -5.471556869451888e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -4.410734163684538e-06}, {"token": "and", "logprob": -0.012388540431857109}, {"token": "died", "logprob": -0.00027044929447583854}, {"token": "in", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": "", "logprob": -2.861018856492592e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.13141779601573944}, {"token": "He", "logprob": 